  "questions": [
    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-37266",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A=\\{3,6,9,12,15,18,21,…\\}?$\n\nI. $B=\\{3n:n\\in N,1\\le n\\le 4\\}$\n\nII. $C=\\{6n:n\\in N,1\\le n\\le 3\\}$\n\nIII. $D=\\{2n:n\\in N,1\\le n\\le 4\\}$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we must find the elements of the sets $B,C$ and $D.$\n\n- We find the elements of the set $B$ substituting the values $n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}$ into the formula $3n,$ and we get the following elements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=1 & \\,⇒\\,3(1)=3 \\\\ n=2 & \\,⇒\\,3(2)=6 \\\\ n=3 & \\,⇒\\,3(3)=9 \\\\ n=4 & \\,⇒\\,3(4)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have, $B=\\{3,6,9,12\\}.$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $C$ can be found substituting the values $n\\in \\{1,2,3\\}$ into the formula $6n,$ and we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=1 & \\,⇒\\,6(1)=6 \\\\ n=2 & \\,⇒\\,6(2)=12 \\\\ n=3 & \\,⇒\\,6(3)=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C=\\{6,12,18\\}.$\n\n- Finally, the elements of the set $D$ can be found substituting the values $n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}$ into the formula $2n,$ and we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=1 & \\,⇒\\,2(1)=2 \\\\ n=2 & \\,⇒\\,2(2)=4 \\\\ n=3 & \\,⇒\\,2(3)=6 \\\\ n=4 & \\,⇒\\,2(4)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have, $D=\\{2,4,6,8\\}.$\n\nNow, a set is a subset of $A$ if all of its elements are also elements of $A.$ With that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- $B=\\{3,6,9,12\\}$ is a subset of $A.\\,✓$\n\n- $C=\\{6,12,18\\}$ is a subset of $A.\\,✓$\n\n- $D=\\{2,4,6,8\\}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element $2\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "III only",
              "option": "0",
