    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-80837",
      "knowledge_point_id": "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying True Statements About Subsets",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in {Z}^{+}∣x\\le 3}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣0\\le x<3}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊈A$\n- $∅⊈B$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all positive integers that satisfy the inequality $x\\le 3.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nA={1,2,3}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $0\\le x<3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={0,1,2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false. For example, $3\\in A$ but $3\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊈A$ is true. For example, $0\\in B$ but $0\\notin A.$\n\n- $∅⊈B$ is false. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "III only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I and III only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "II only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I and II only"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "II only",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "correct_origin": "model_interpretation"
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "provenance": {
        "activity_question": "question-80837",
        "kp_title": "Identifying True Statements About Subsets",
        "kp_href": null
      }
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-1210",
      "knowledge_point_id": "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying True Statements About Subsets",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z:-2\\le x<6}$ and $B={x\\in N:x+1<6}?$\n\n- $∅⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊈B$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all integer numbers greater than or equal to $-2$ but less than $6.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $x+1<6.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $∅⊆B$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $-1\\in A$ but $-1\\notin B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\".",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "III only"
            },
