    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-1212",
      "knowledge_point_id": "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying True Statements About Subsets",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in N∣-1\\le x+1\\le 3}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣{x}^{2}\\le -2}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $A⊈B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊇B$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $-1\\le x+1\\le 3,$ that is to say, $-2\\le x\\le 2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={1,2}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}\\le -2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB=∅.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false, while $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $0\\in A$ but $0\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. The empty set is a subset of every set.\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II, III, and IV only.\"",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
